import DocumentLayout from "../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro";
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Using Locus"
  description="A quick guide to organizing PDFs, reading, and drawing in Locus."
>
  <h1>A place to read.<br>A place to work things out.</h1>
  <p>
    Locus is a desktop workspace for independent study. Put the book beside a drawing canvas, work
    through an idea, and return to the same material later.
  </p>
  <h2>Start with a workspace</h2>
  <p>
    Create a workspace for a subject, project, or question. Add existing PDFs directly, or organize
    them into folders. These are references to the originals: adding a book doesn’t move or
    duplicate it.
  </p>
  <h2>Read at your own pace</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Scroll through the whole PDF vertically.</li>
    <li>Enter a page number and press Enter to jump.</li>
    <li>Choose a zoom level, fit to width, or fit to height.</li>
    <li>Hold Ctrl and scroll to zoom around the pointer.</li>
    <li>Click a document link to navigate; web links open in your browser.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Locus saves your reading position. If you move the original PDF elsewhere, use the locate-file
    option to reconnect it.
  </p>
  <h2>Keep a drawing beside the source</h2>
  <p>
    Create an Excalidraw whiteboard for sketches, handwritten working, or diagrams. Drawings save
    locally as standard <code>.excalidraw</code> files.
  </p>
  <p>
    Precise pins that connect a drawing to a location in the PDF are planned, but are not available
    yet. For now, give each whiteboard a name that helps you find it again.
  </p>
  <h2>Arrange your space</h2>
  <p>
    Locus remembers the window size and whether it was maximized when you close it normally. Window
    placement follows your desktop’s rules; Wayland controls its own placement.
  </p>
  <p>
    Split vertically or horizontally, up to four panes, and resize the space between them. A new
    split starts empty. You can open the same PDF in more than one pane and read at different
    positions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The title bar shows which pane is active. Use the command palette to find actions, or set
    shortcuts in Preferences. Ctrl+W closes the active pane; closing the last document leaves an
    empty workspace.
  </p>
  <h2>Make room for the working</h2>
  <p>
    While a whiteboard is active, Alt+P hides or shows its controls. You can change that shortcut
    and the other key bindings in Preferences. Light and dark themes are available.
  </p>
  <p>
    Search settings by name or choose a category under Workbench or Application. Use Modified to
    find settings you changed. Appearance groups themes into light and dark palettes. Choose a
    built-in palette or import a Locus theme JSON file. Open the config folder to edit your
    settings, key bindings, or theme files under Settings → Configuration, and Locus detects theme
    changes automatically while visible. Use Reload configuration for changes to preferences or key
    bindings. You can also search for Color Theme in the command palette and choose from the light
    and dark groups. Existing preferences migrate when native files are missing.
  </p>
  <h2>Your files stay yours</h2>
  <p>
    Removing a PDF from Locus removes its reference, not the original file. Saved drawings also
    remain on disk when their entries are removed. The app requires no account, AI subscription, or
    cloud service.
  </p>
  <p>
    Locus is still in early development. Read the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> for
    implemented changes and the <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a> for work that remains.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
